import{_ as o,o as t,c as d,ao as a}from"./chunks/framework.BQjsKH0C.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Operation contracts","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"operations.md","filePath":"operations.md","lastUpdated":null}'),c={name:"operations.md"};function n(r,e,i,s,l,p){return t(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="Operation-contracts" tabindex="-1">Operation contracts <a class="header-anchor" href="#Operation-contracts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Operation contracts {#Operation-contracts}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page describes the Julia interface. An operation in tenferro-rs does not automatically imply the same operation is exposed by Tenferro.jl.</p><h2 id="Types-and-devices" tabindex="-1">Types and devices <a class="header-anchor" href="#Types-and-devices" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Types and devices {#Types-and-devices}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>AutoTenferro()</code> and <code>AutoTenferroTraced()</code> accept CPU arrays of <code>Float32</code>, <code>Float64</code>, <code>ComplexF32</code>, and <code>ComplexF64</code>. Integer and <code>Bool</code> arrays are storage and forward-computation types, not differentiable inputs. GPU construction, device transfer, and GPU execution are not exposed by this Julia package. Converting a GPU array through the <code>AbstractArray</code> constructor would copy to the host; it does not select a GPU backend.</p><p><code>TypedTensor</code> provides storage and Julia&#39;s <code>AbstractArray</code> fallback operations. Those fallback operations are not recorded for Tenferro AD. Use <code>EagerTensor</code> or <code>TracedTensor</code> for the Rust operation and AD surfaces described here.</p><h2 id="Arithmetic-and-broadcasting" tabindex="-1">Arithmetic and broadcasting <a class="header-anchor" href="#Arithmetic-and-broadcasting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Arithmetic and broadcasting {#Arithmetic-and-broadcasting}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>A + B</code> and <code>A - B</code> require equal shapes. <code>A * B</code> is matrix multiplication (or a matrix–vector product), whereas <code>A .* B</code> is elementwise multiplication. Dot operations support Julia&#39;s leading-axis broadcast convention: missing trailing dimensions have extent one. The Julia adapter explicitly expands operands before calling Rust, so Rust&#39;s own implicit broadcast convention is not used. Cotangents of expanded axes are summed back to each input&#39;s shape.</p><p>Binary dot operations are <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code>, <code>^</code>, <code>rem</code> (also <code>.%</code>), <code>min</code>, and <code>max</code>. Scalars and constant Julia arrays can appear on either side. Mixed floating-point and complex operands use Julia&#39;s <code>promote_type</code>; cotangents are converted back to each active input&#39;s type. Unsupported promoted types are rejected. Tensors in one operation must share a context.</p><p><code>+</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>min</code> broadcast over more than two arguments as Julia&#39;s left fold, for example <code>max.(x, y, 0)</code>, and <code>muladd.(a, b, c)</code> and <code>fma.(a, b, c)</code> are <code>a .* b .+ c</code>. Partially applied functions with a scalar, such as <code>(&gt;(0)).(x)</code> or <code>Base.Fix1(-, 1).(x)</code>, broadcast as the binary operation. <code>zero.(x)</code> and <code>one.(x)</code> are constant tensors. <code>similar</code> throws an <code>ArgumentError</code>: tensors are immutable.</p><p>Same-type <code>Int32</code>/<code>Int64</code> tensors support forward <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, elementwise <code>*</code>, <code>rem</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>abs</code>, and <code>sign</code>. Elementwise <code>/</code> converts integer and Boolean operands to <code>Float64</code>, matching Julia rather than Rust&#39;s integer division. Mixed integer tensor types and integer type promotion are not a supported general interface. Boolean arithmetic is otherwise rejected by Rust.</p><p><code>clamp.(x, lo, hi)</code> is composed as <code>min.(max.(x, lo), hi)</code> and allows broadcast bounds. <code>abs.</code>, <code>sign.</code>, <code>conj.</code>, and unary <code>-</code> are elementwise operations. Complex <code>abs</code> returns the corresponding real element type. Ordering operations (<code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>clamp</code>, <code>minimum</code>, <code>maximum</code>) are restricted to real values.</p><p>Derivatives at quotient discontinuities of <code>rem</code>, zero of <code>abs</code>/<code>sign</code>, power branch points, or equal operands of <code>min</code>/<code>max</code> are not classical derivatives. <code>rem</code> uses derivatives <code>1</code> and <code>-div(x,y)</code> away from its discontinuities; its divisor must be nonzero. Complex <code>sign</code> uses the real derivative of <code>z / abs(z)</code> away from zero. Do not use finite differences across these discontinuities as a gradient reference.</p><h2 id="Element-type-casts-and-constants" tabindex="-1">Element-type casts and constants <a class="header-anchor" href="#Element-type-casts-and-constants" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Element-type casts and constants {#Element-type-casts-and-constants}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>S.(x)</code> casts a tensor to any tensor element type <code>S</code> (<code>Float32</code>, <code>Float64</code>, <code>ComplexF32</code>, <code>ComplexF64</code>, <code>Int32</code>, <code>Int64</code>, <code>Bool</code>) with the tenferro-rs <code>cast</code>, in both directions, for example <code>Float32.(x)</code> of a <code>Float64</code> tensor, <code>ComplexF64.(x)</code> of a real tensor, or <code>Float32.(x .&gt; 0)</code> of a mask; <code>float.(x)</code> casts to the floating-point type of <code>eltype(x)</code>. Casts between floating-point and complex types are differentiable: cotangents are cast back to the input type. Casts to or from integer and <code>Bool</code> types carry no derivative. Floating-point values cast to integers truncate toward zero, any nonzero value casts to <code>true</code>, and integer narrowing wraps, unlike Julia&#39;s <code>InexactError</code>. A complex tensor cannot be cast to a real type, which would drop the imaginary part (Julia throws <code>InexactError</code> when it is nonzero); <code>real.(z)</code> and <code>imag.(z)</code> project it differentiably. <code>convert(EagerTensor{Float32}, x)</code> (and likewise for <code>TracedTensor</code>) is the same cast and returns <code>x</code> itself when it already has the requested type. Mixed-type arithmetic uses the same cast for promotion.</p><p><code>Tenferro.context(x)</code> returns the <code>EagerContext</code> or <code>TracedContext</code> that owns <code>x</code>, and <code>Tenferro.constant(x, array)</code> copies a Julia array or number into it as a constant, without derivatives, keeping its element type. Inside a function differentiated by <code>AutoTenferro()</code> or <code>AutoTenferroTraced()</code>, this is how to create tensors that combine with the traced input.</p><h2 id="Comparisons-and-selection" tabindex="-1">Comparisons and selection <a class="header-anchor" href="#Comparisons-and-selection" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Comparisons and selection {#Comparisons-and-selection}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>.==</code>, <code>.!=</code>, <code>.&lt;</code>, <code>.&lt;=</code>, <code>.&gt;</code> and <code>.&gt;=</code> compare tensors, scalars and constant Julia arrays with the broadcast and promotion rules above and return <code>Bool</code> tensors. Comparisons are not differentiable: no cotangent flows through them. <code>.!</code> negates a <code>Bool</code> tensor. <code>ifelse.(mask, a, b)</code> selects elementwise; the mask may be a <code>Bool</code> tensor, a <code>Bool</code> array or a <code>Bool</code>, and the branches may be tensors, scalars or constant arrays of a common promoted type. Cotangents flow into the selected branch only. <code>abs2.(x)</code> and <code>inv.(x)</code> are composed from the supported arithmetic operations.</p><h2 id="Elementwise-mathematical-functions" tabindex="-1">Elementwise mathematical functions <a class="header-anchor" href="#Elementwise-mathematical-functions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Elementwise mathematical functions {#Elementwise-mathematical-functions}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The eight supported broadcast functions call the corresponding Rust operations:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Julia</th><th style="text-align:right;">Derivative away from poles and branch cuts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>exp.(x)</code>, <code>expm1.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>exp.(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>log.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1 ./ x</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>log1p.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1 ./ (1 .+ x)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sqrt.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1 ./ (2 .* sqrt.(x))</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sin.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>cos.(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>cos.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>-sin.(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>tanh.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1 .- tanh.(x).^2</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Complex VJPs conjugate the analytic derivative. Tests compare both precisions and complex types against Julia values, analytic derivatives, and directional finite differences away from singularities. <code>sqrt</code> at zero, <code>log</code> at zero, and <code>log1p</code> at minus one have singular derivatives. Complex branch cuts and poles are excluded from differentiability claims. Real input is not implicitly promoted to complex to extend a function&#39;s domain.</p><p>Real negative arguments of <code>log</code> and <code>sqrt</code>, and arguments below minus one of <code>log1p</code>, currently produce Rust&#39;s IEEE <code>NaN</code>, whereas Julia Base throws a <code>DomainError</code>. <code>log(0)</code> and <code>log1p(-1)</code> produce <code>-Inf</code>. Integer and Boolean inputs to these eight mathematical functions are rejected rather than implicitly converted to floating-point; explicitly convert the input first.</p><p><code>exp(A)</code> is a matrix function and is not provided by these elementwise methods. Loading SpecialFunctions.jl enables <code>erf.(x)</code> for real tensors through a package extension.</p><h2 id="Broadcast-fallback" tabindex="-1">Broadcast fallback <a class="header-anchor" href="#Broadcast-fallback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Broadcast fallback {#Broadcast-fallback}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Only the functions listed in this guide are lowered to tenferro-rs. Broadcasting any other function over a tensor, including an anonymous function or a closure, throws an <code>ArgumentError</code> that names the function; write its body as separate dotted operations. <code>(f ∘ g).(x...)</code> is expanded to <code>f.(g.(x...))</code>, and <code>identity.(x)</code> returns <code>x</code>. An <code>EagerScalar</code> broadcasts with tensors, numbers and constant arrays.</p><h2 id="Neural-network-operations" tabindex="-1">Neural-network operations <a class="header-anchor" href="#Neural-network-operations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Neural-network operations {#Neural-network-operations}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These operations are available after <code>using NNlib</code> (package extension <code>TenferroNNlibExt</code>), for eager and traced tensors, and are differentiable by both backends.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">NNlib function</th><th style="text-align:right;">Implementation and derivative at kinks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sigmoid</code>/<code>σ</code>, <code>sigmoid_fast</code>, <code>softplus</code>, <code>swish</code>, <code>gelu</code>/<code>gelu_tanh</code>, <code>gelu_erf</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">tenferro-rs activation kernels (overflow-free <code>sigmoid</code> and <code>softplus</code>)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>relu</code>, <code>relu6</code>, <code>trelu</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>select</code>; derivative <code>0</code> at every kink</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>leakyrelu(x, a=0.01)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>select</code>; derivative <code>a</code> at <code>0</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>elu(x, α=1)</code>, <code>celu(x, α=1)</code>, <code>selu</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>select</code> with <code>expm1</code>; <code>elu&#39;(0) == celu&#39;(0) == 1</code>, <code>selu&#39;(0) == λα</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>hardσ</code>, <code>hardtanh</code>, <code>softshrink(x, λ=0.5)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>select</code>; derivative <code>0</code> at the kinks</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>hardswish</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>x .* hardσ.(x)</code> with NNlib&#39;s derivative <code>x/3 + 1/2</code> on <code>[-3, 3]</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>logσ</code>, <code>softsign</code>, <code>mish</code>, <code>lisht</code>, <code>tanhshrink</code>, <code>logcosh</code>, <code>tanh_fast</code>, <code>gelu_sigmoid</code>, <code>quick_gelu</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">composed from the smooth operations above</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The activations accept <code>Float32</code> and <code>Float64</code> tensors, and eager scalars. <code>leakyrelu</code>, <code>elu</code>, <code>celu</code>, <code>softshrink</code> and <code>trelu</code> also accept their parameter as a constant real number, as in <code>leakyrelu.(x, 0.2)</code>. <code>rrelu</code> (random) is not provided. <code>NNlib.fast_act(f, x)</code> returns <code>f</code> for tensors. Unlike NNlib&#39;s <code>tanh_fast</code> and <code>sigmoid_fast</code>, the tensor versions are not approximations, so <code>Float32</code> results can differ from NNlib&#39;s in the last few bits.</p><p><code>softmax(x; dims=1)</code>, <code>logsoftmax(x; dims=1)</code> and <code>logsumexp(x; dims=:)</code> accept an integer, a tuple of dimensions or <code>:</code>. For a single dimension, softmax and logsoftmax use the tenferro-rs kernels; otherwise they are composed from <code>maximum</code>, <code>exp</code>, <code>sum</code> and <code>log</code>, shifted by the maximum along <code>dims</code>.</p><p><code>batched_mul(A, B)</code> (<code>A ⊠ B</code>) multiplies the matrices <code>A[:, :, k]</code> and <code>B[:, :, k]</code> through tenferro-rs <code>dot_general</code> with a batch dimension. It follows NNlib: a 3-D operand with batch size one is reused for every batch, a matrix operand multiplies every batch, and higher-rank operands with equal trailing dimensions are reshaped to one batch dimension. Either operand may be a constant Julia array. <code>batched_transpose</code> and <code>batched_adjoint</code> return permuted (and, for complex tensors, conjugated) tensors, not lazy wrappers.</p><p><code>pad_zeros(x, pad; dims=:)</code> and <code>pad_constant(x, pad, val=0; dims=:)</code> follow NNlib&#39;s padding arguments: an integer, one amount per padded dimension, a <code>(low, high)</code> amount pair per padded dimension, or a tuple of <code>(low, high)</code> pairs. They use tenferro-rs <code>pad</code>; a nonzero <code>val</code> adds a constant frame. Negative padding (cropping) is not supported.</p><h3 id="Convolution,-pooling-and-upsampling" tabindex="-1">Convolution, pooling and upsampling <a class="header-anchor" href="#Convolution,-pooling-and-upsampling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Convolution, pooling and upsampling {#Convolution,-pooling-and-upsampling}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>tenferro-rs has no convolution or <code>reduce_window</code> primitive, so these functions are composed from its <code>pad</code>, <code>slice</code>, <code>concatenate</code>, <code>dot_general</code>, reduction and <code>select</code> kernels. They follow NNlib&#39;s WHCN layout, <code>(spatial..., channels, batch)</code> with one to three spatial dimensions, and NNlib&#39;s argument conventions; gradients are those of the composed kernels.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">NNlib function</th><th style="text-align:right;">Implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>conv(x, w; stride, pad, dilation, flipped, groups)</code>, <code>conv(x, w, ::DenseConvDims)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">zero <code>pad</code>, one strided <code>slice</code> per kernel offset concatenated into windows (im2col), then one <code>dot_general</code> over kernel offsets and input channels; groups are a <code>dot_general</code> batch dimension</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>depthwiseconv(x, w; ...)</code>, <code>depthwiseconv(x, w, ::DepthwiseConvDims)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">grouped <code>conv</code> with <code>groups = C_in</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>∇conv_data(dy, w, cdims)</code>, <code>∇depthwiseconv_data</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">transposed convolution: <code>conv</code> of <code>dy</code> with interior (stride) padding and the channel-swapped kernel reversed once more</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>∇conv_filter(x, dy, cdims)</code>, <code>∇depthwiseconv_filter</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">windows of <code>x</code> contracted with <code>dy</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>maxpool(x, k; pad, stride, ceil_mode)</code>, <code>maxpool(x, ::PoolDims)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">running maximum over one strided slice per kernel offset of the input padded with <code>-Inf</code>, updated by <code>select</code> only where an entry is strictly larger, so each window&#39;s gradient goes to its first maximum in kernel order, as in NNlib&#39;s <code>∇maxpool</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>meanpool(x, k; pad, stride, count_include_pad, ceil_mode)</code>, <code>meanpool(x, ::PoolDims)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">window sums divided by NNlib&#39;s per-window count: all window positions inside the padded input, or only those inside the input with <code>count_include_pad=false</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>lpnormpool(x, p, k; pad, stride, ceil_mode)</code>, <code>lpnormpool(x, ::PoolDims; p)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(∑ xᵢ^p)^(1/p)</code> over each window (padding contributes nothing)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>upsample_nearest(x, scales)</code>, <code>upsample_nearest(x; size)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>broadcast_to</code> of a reshaped tensor</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>upsample_linear</code>, <code>upsample_bilinear</code>, <code>upsample_trilinear</code> (scale or <code>size</code>, both <code>align_corners</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;">one pair of axis <code>gather</code>s with constant interpolation weights per resized dimension (NNlib&#39;s source positions)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>pixel_shuffle(x, r)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>reshape</code> and <code>permutedims</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>PoolDims</code>, <code>DenseConvDims</code> and <code>DepthwiseConvDims</code> accept tensors in place of arrays. Either operand of <code>conv</code>, <code>depthwiseconv</code>, <code>∇conv_data</code> and <code>∇conv_filter</code> may be a constant Julia array. <code>maximum</code> and <code>minimum</code> accept <code>init</code> (Lux&#39;s <code>GlobalMaxPool</code> calls <code>maximum(x; dims, init=-Inf)</code>), so Lux&#39;s global and adaptive pooling layers work. Convolution windows are built from <code>sum(kernel)</code> slices and concatenations (one per kernel offset and dimension); mean and Lp pooling sum <code>prod(kernel)</code> output-sized slices, or reshape and reduce when the windows do not overlap (stride equal to the kernel size). Anti-aliased bilinear upsampling, <code>upsample_bicubic</code> and complex inputs are not provided.</p><p><code>NNlib.gather(src, idx)</code> (and <code>gather(src, I, J, ...)</code>) accepts integers, integer tuples and <code>CartesianIndex</code>es as indices: one axis gather of <code>src</code> with the indexed trailing dimensions flattened. <code>NNlib.scatter(op, src, idx; init, dstsize)</code> supports <code>op</code> in <code>+</code>, <code>-</code> and <code>Statistics.mean</code>, built on the additive tenferro-rs <code>scatter</code>; repeated indices accumulate. Other operators (<code>max</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code>) throw an <code>ArgumentError</code>. Indices are checked on the host; out-of-range indices throw a <code>BoundsError</code> instead of being clamped.</p><h2 id="Reductions" tabindex="-1">Reductions <a class="header-anchor" href="#Reductions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reductions {#Reductions}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>sum</code>, <code>prod</code>, <code>maximum</code>, <code>minimum</code>, and <code>Statistics.mean</code> accept <code>dims</code>; <code>prod</code>, <code>maximum</code> and <code>minimum</code> also accept <code>init</code>, combined with the result by one more <code>*</code>, <code>max</code> or <code>min</code>. <code>Statistics.var</code> and <code>Statistics.std</code> accept <code>dims</code>, <code>corrected</code> and a precomputed <code>mean</code>; they are composed from <code>mean</code>, <code>abs2</code> and <code>sum</code>. <code>sum(f, x)</code>, <code>prod(f, x)</code>, <code>maximum(f, x)</code>, <code>minimum(f, x)</code> and <code>Statistics.mean(f, x)</code> reduce <code>f.(x)</code>, with the same keywords; <code>f</code> must be a supported elementwise function such as <code>abs2</code>. Without <code>dims</code>, they reduce all elements to a scalar. With <code>dims</code>, Julia dimensions are converted from one-based to zero-based Rust axes; reduced dimensions are restored with extent one. Tuples of axes may be unordered. Duplicate or nonpositive axes are rejected; axes beyond the input rank are ignored. An empty tuple of axes returns the input unchanged.</p><p>Product derivatives account for zero factors: one zero receives the product of the other factors, and two or more zeros give zero derivatives. Tied reduction extrema divide the cotangent equally among the tied elements. This is a selected subgradient, not differentiability at a tie. It differs from <a href="https://github.com/JuliaDiff/ChainRules.jl/blob/main/src/rulesets/Base/array.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ChainRules&#39; extrema rules</a>, which route the seed to the single index selected by <code>findmin</code>/<code>findmax</code>. For example, the gradient of <code>minimum([1,1,2])</code> here is <code>[0.5,0.5,0]</code>, whereas ChainRules selects <code>[1,0,0]</code>. Product zero-factor handling agrees with the <a href="https://github.com/JuliaDiff/ChainRules.jl/blob/main/src/rulesets/Base/mapreduce.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ChainRules product rule</a>. These comparisons do not install or invoke ChainRules during Tenferro AD. Complex <code>sum</code>, <code>prod</code>, and <code>mean</code> are supported; complex extrema are not.</p><p>On zero-length reduced axes, the Julia wrapper supplies the sum/product identities, <code>mean</code> returns <code>NaN</code>, and <code>maximum</code>/<code>minimum</code> throw as in Julia. The backend identity extension is tracked in <a href="https://github.com/tensor4all/tenferro-rs/issues/2453" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">tenferro-rs#2453</a>. A zero-length <em>non-reduced</em> dimension gives an empty output with the expected shape. An empty input is distinct from an empty tuple of reduction axes. The dtype and AD guarantees in this section apply to floating/complex types; integer widening (for example Julia&#39;s <code>sum(::Vector{Int32})</code>) is not a supported Julia-compatible contract.</p><p><code>Bool</code> tensors reduce like Julia&#39;s: <code>sum(mask)</code> and <code>count(mask)</code> (also <code>count(f, x)</code>) return <code>Int64</code> counts, <code>mean(mask)</code> and the mean of an integer tensor are <code>Float64</code>, and <code>any(mask)</code>, <code>all(mask)</code>, <code>any(f, x)</code> and <code>all(f, x)</code> return <code>Bool</code>, all with optional <code>dims</code>. Without <code>dims</code> the eager result is a scalar tensor, not a Julia number: use <code>only(any(mask))</code> or <code>Bool(any(mask))</code> to branch on it. A traced result is a zero-dimensional traced tensor.</p><h2 id="Shapes,-indexing,-and-ownership" tabindex="-1">Shapes, indexing, and ownership <a class="header-anchor" href="#Shapes,-indexing,-and-ownership" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Shapes, indexing, and ownership {#Shapes,-indexing,-and-ownership}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>reshape</code> follows Julia column-major element order. <code>permutedims</code> accepts a permutation of one-based axes. A vector&#39;s <code>transpose</code> or <code>adjoint</code> has shape <code>(1,n)</code>; a matrix swaps its two axes. <code>adjoint</code> also conjugates values and its pullback conjugates the seed; <code>transpose</code> does not conjugate. Higher-rank <code>transpose</code> is rejected; use <code>permutedims</code> instead.</p><p><code>reverse</code> accepts one dimension or a tuple of dimensions. <code>cat</code> accepts one positive dimension; lower-rank operands get trailing singleton dimensions, so <code>cat(a, b; dims=3)</code> of matrices or <code>hcat</code> of vectors works as in Julia. Every other axis must have equal extents; floating-point/complex types are promoted. <code>vcat</code> and <code>hcat</code> of tensors are <code>cat</code> along dimensions 1 and 2. Constant Julia arrays may be mixed with tensors in <code>cat</code>, <code>vcat</code> and <code>hcat</code> (the first tensor must be among the first three arguments), for example <code>hcat(x, ones(3, 1))</code>. <code>vec(x)</code> flattens to a vector, and <code>repeat(x, counts...)</code> and <code>repeat(x; inner, outer)</code> follow Base with positive counts, as a reshape and <code>broadcast_to</code> whose cotangents sum over the repeated entries. Block concatenation tuples are not exposed. <code>dropdims</code>, <code>selectdim</code>, and <code>eachslice</code> (returning a Julia array of tensor slices, with <code>drop</code>) follow Base.</p><p>Indexing supports one integer, nonempty positive-step integer range, <code>:</code>, nonempty integer vector, or Boolean mask per dimension, for example <code>W[:, [1, 3, 3]]</code>. Integer dimensions are dropped. Integer vectors gather along their dimension; repeated indices accumulate their cotangents. Empty ranges and negative steps are explicitly rejected; <code>A[:]</code> flattens. Linear indexing of matrices, Cartesian index arrays, <code>view</code>, and <code>setindex!</code> are not exposed. <code>tril</code>, <code>triu</code>, and nonempty <code>diag</code> use Julia&#39;s signed diagonal offsets.</p><p>Input construction and <code>Array(tensor)</code> each copy data. Raw Julia pointers are used only during the FFI call under <code>GC.@preserve</code>; Rust copies input buffers before returning. A tensor retains its shared context, so the context variable need not remain in the caller&#39;s scope. Rust may share immutable storage for shape/view operations, but no mutable Julia alias or write-through view is returned. Mutating an input Julia array or a materialized result does not modify a tensor. Explicitly closing a context invalidates every tensor that shares it. Context accesses are serialized by a Julia lock.</p><h2 id="Linear-algebra" tabindex="-1">Linear algebra <a class="header-anchor" href="#Linear-algebra" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Linear algebra {#Linear-algebra}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Julia decomposition results are lightweight Tenferro containers. They are not subtypes of Julia&#39;s standard factorization types and do not provide all their methods or keyword options. For an <code>m × n</code> matrix and <code>k = min(m,n)</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Operation</th><th style="text-align:right;">Result contract</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>svd(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Thin <code>U: (m,k)</code>, real <code>S: (k,)</code>, <code>Vt: (k,n)</code>; <code>A ≈ U * Diagonal(S) * Vt</code>; <code>.V</code> is <code>Vt&#39;</code>; iteration gives <code>U,S,V</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>qr(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Unpivoted thin <code>Q: (m,k)</code>, <code>R: (k,n)</code>; <code>A ≈ Q*R</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>lu(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>P: (m,m)</code>, <code>L: (m,k)</code>, <code>U: (k,n)</code>; <code>P*A ≈ L*U</code>; no <code>.p</code> or Julia pivot-vector contract</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>cholesky(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Lower factor <code>.L</code>, conjugate transpose <code>.U</code>; <code>A ≈ L*U</code>; checks Hermitian input</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eigen(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>.values</code>, <code>.vectors</code>; general eigenvalues and eigenvectors are complex even for real input</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eigen(Hermitian(A,uplo))</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Real eigenvalues; uses only the selected triangle, with real diagonal</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>A \\ B</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Square solve, or least squares for rectangular <code>A</code>; accepts vector or matrix right-hand side</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>LowerTriangular(A) \\ B</code>, <code>UpperTriangular(A) \\ B</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Uses the selected triangle</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>det</code>, <code>inv</code>, <code>pinv</code>, <code>norm</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Scalar determinant, inverse, pseudoinverse, and Euclidean/Frobenius norm</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Eigenvalues use Julia-compatible sorting by default; <code>sortby=nothing</code> retains backend order. Decomposition signs and complex phases are not fixed to match Julia; compare reconstructions, invariant quantities, or consistently aligned factors. Batch dimensions, pivoted QR, full SVD, configurable pseudoinverse tolerances, and general norm orders are not exposed. Full SVD is explicitly rejected because Rust does not supply its AD rule.</p><p>The adapter propagates numerical failures from Rust. Singular solves/inverses, nonpositive Cholesky inputs, nonconvergence, changing rank, repeated singular or eigenvalues, zero norm, and pivot changes may fail or have undefined or ill-conditioned derivatives. These are not covered by smooth-gradient claims. The default <code>pinv</code> rank threshold belongs to Rust; it is not promised to equal Julia&#39;s default threshold at a numerical rank boundary.</p>',52)])])}const u=o(c,[["render",n]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
